If you unwind adhesive tape from a nonrotating circuolar tape dispenser so that the unwound tape is held taut and tangent to the dispensor roll, then the end of the tape traces a curve called the involute of the circle. 
Lets find the parametric equatiojns for this curve, assuming that the dispensing roll has constant radius a and is centred at the orgin. 
Considering Figure 1.29, we see that the position vector OP-> of the desired point P is the vecotr sum OB-> + BP->. 
To determine OB-> as our parameter. 
Since B is a point on the circle. 

	OB-> = acos(θ)i + asin(θ)j

To find the vector BP->, parallel translate it so that its tail is at the orgin. Figur 1.30 shows that BP->'s length musht be aθ, the amount of unwond tape, and its direction must be such that it makes an angle of θ-Π/2 with the positive x-axis. 
From our experience with circular geometry and, perhaps, polar coordinates, we see that BP is described by 

	BP-> = aΘcos(Θ-π/2)i + aθsin(Θ-π/2)j = aΘsin(Θ)i - aΘcos(Θ)j

Hence, 

	OP-> = OB-> + BP-> = a(cos(Θ) + Θsin(Θ))i + a(sin(Θ) - Θcos(θ))j

